---
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import ClaimBlock from '../components/ClaimBlock.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { release, url } from '../lib/publication';
const claim = (id: string) => release.claims.find(c => c.id === id);
const source = (id: string) => {
  const item = claim(id);
  return item ? url(`/sources/${item.evidenceIds[0]}/`) : url('/sources/');
};
const forecast = claim('C-IMD-OUTLOOK-20260831');
const rain = claim('C-IMD-RAINFALL-DEP-india-20260923');
const storage = claim('C-CWC-INDIA-STORAGE-20260910');
const storageSouth = claim('C-CWC-SOUTH-STORAGE-20260910');
const karnataka = claim('C-KA-DROUGHT-ORDER-20260922');
const heavyRain = claim('C-IMD-WEATHER-EVENT-20260924');
const weekForecast = claim('C-IMD-EXTENDED-20260917');
const assessment = claim('C-IWW-AI-ASSESSMENT-20260924');
---
<Site title="What happens next?" description="A low-confidence AI Author assessment, dated official forecast and falsifiable recovery signals for India's water systems.">
  <PageHeader title="The next chapter is not written" eyebrow="Outlook / distinct clocks" description="A forecast concerns future rain. An observation measures past rain or storage. Our assessment is a conditional judgment, not an official warning or a model probability." />
  <section class="section g11-chapter"><div class="shell"><div class="g11-chapter-head"><span class="g11-chapter-number">01</span><div><p class="eyebrow">What is on the record</p><h2>Different dates. Different kinds of evidence.</h2></div></div>
    <div class="g11-signal-grid"><article class="g11-signal"><span class="g11-tag measured">Measured · IMD reported</span><strong>−14.9%</strong><p>All-India rain from 1 June–23 September 2026 versus the normal for those dates. It does not measure supply.</p><a href={rain ? source(rain.id) : url('/sources/')}>23 September graphic ↗</a></article><article class="g11-signal"><span class="g11-tag measured">Measured · CWC reported</span><strong>70.66%</strong><p>of combined live capacity in 178 monitored reservoirs on 10 September. It is an earlier snapshot, not a trend.</p><a href={storage ? source(storage.id) : url('/sources/')}>10 September bulletin ↗</a></article><article class="g11-signal"><span class="g11-tag order">Government order</span><strong>53</strong><p>additional Karnataka taluks declared drought-affected on 22 September. The order authorizes relief and directs a survey; it does not document delivery or loss.</p><a href={karnataka ? source(karnataka.id) : url('/sources/')}>22 September gazette ↗</a></article></div>
    {heavyRain && <aside class="g11-clock"><span class="g11-tag measured">Observed event · IMD reported</span><h3>The next morning brought a different signal.</h3><p>For the 24 hours ending <strong>08:30 IST on 24 September</strong>, IMD reported extreme rain in coastal Andhra Pradesh and very heavy rain in Odisha, Jharkhand, coastal and south-interior Karnataka, and Telangana. This can coexist with a cumulative shortfall through 23 September. It cannot yet tell us whether the relevant catchments, reservoirs or taps recovered. <a href={source(heavyRain.id)}>IMD weather release ↗</a></p></aside>}
    <p class="small muted">The CWC and IMD measures have different units, denominators, dates and boundaries. CWC's 50 southern reservoirs are not IMD's South Peninsular rainfall region. <a href={storageSouth ? source(storageSouth.id) : url('/methodology/')}>Southern storage cohort ↗</a></p>
  </div></section>

  <section class="section g11-chapter"><div class="shell"><div class="g11-chapter-head"><span class="g11-chapter-number">02</span><div><p class="eyebrow">Our published judgment</p><h2>What we expect before the next monsoon.</h2></div></div>
    <article class="g11-assessment"><span class="g11-tag ai-assessment">AI Author assessment</span><p class="eyebrow">Issued 24 September 2026 · low confidence · qualitative horizon: before the 2027 southwest monsoon</p><h3>Expect a patchwork, not a single national water outcome.</h3><p>Our working projection is that some local systems may remain under pressure through the coming dry season while others replenish. The mixed broad rainfall departures, earlier reservoir snapshot, bounded Karnataka declaration and later local heavy rain warrant close monitoring. They do <strong>not</strong> identify which district's taps will fail, how many households will be affected or when a shortage would begin.</p><div class="grid-2"><div><h4>What this judgment assumes</h4><p>Dry-season withdrawals continue; replenishment differs among catchments; and storage, groundwater and delivery do not respond to a national rainfall average as one system. These are working assumptions, not measured 2026 outcomes.</p></div><div><h4>Why confidence is low</h4><p>Only one approved CWC storage date is available here, with no matched post-rainfall trend, current groundwater network or local utility service series. The 10 September reservoir status cannot establish conditions at the 23 September rain cutoff or after the 24 September storm.</p></div></div><p class="small">Starting evidence: <a href={rain ? source(rain.id) : url('/sources/')}>IMD cumulative rainfall ↗</a> · <a href={heavyRain ? source(heavyRain.id) : url('/sources/')}>IMD weather event ↗</a> · <a href={storage ? source(storage.id) : url('/sources/')}>CWC storage ↗</a> · <a href={karnataka ? source(karnataka.id) : url('/sources/')}>Karnataka order ↗</a>. This is AI-authored inference, not an IMD/CWC forecast or a validated statistical model.</p>{assessment && <p class="small">Claim ID: {assessment.id} · <a href={url('/sources/E-IMD-ALLINDIA-20260923/#C-IWW-AI-ASSESSMENT-20260924')}>Read this AI assessment’s evidence and limits ↗</a></p>}</article>
  </div></section>

  <section class="section g11-chapter"><div class="shell"><div class="g11-chapter-head"><span class="g11-chapter-number">03</span><div><p class="eyebrow">What would change our mind</p><h2>The test is in the next observations.</h2></div></div>
    <div class="grid-2"><article class="g11-branch"><p class="eyebrow">Concern would strengthen if</p><h3>A named supply system shows a chain of stress.</h3><p>Catchment rain remains low; a stable reservoir cohort or covered well network fails to recover; local service records show repeated interruptions or failed quality tests. These must refer to the same service area and dated period. The current evidence does not establish that chain.</p></article><article class="g11-branch"><p class="eyebrow">Concern would weaken if</p><h3>Replenishment reaches people.</h3><p>Matched catchment rain, live storage and groundwater improve, and utility records show reliable quantity and quality in the affected area. A single storm or a full reservoir elsewhere would not suffice.</p></article></div>
    <div class="figure"><p class="eyebrow">Falsifiable watch list</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Next record required</th><th scope="col">Current limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Did sources refill?</td><td>Matched catchment rain and repeat CWC storage for a stable reservoir cohort.</td><td>One approved <a href={storage ? source(storage.id) : url('/sources/')}>10 September bulletin</a>; no approved current trend.</td></tr><tr><td>Did groundwater recover?</td><td>Dated wells, network coverage and comparable depths.</td><td>No approved current groundwater series.</td></tr><tr><td>Did service improve or worsen?</td><td>Utility hours, volume and quality tests in the same service area.</td><td>No approved September 2026 household-service measure.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="section g11-chapter"><div class="shell"><div class="g11-chapter-head"><span class="g11-chapter-number">04</span><div><p class="eyebrow">Separate from our assessment</p><h2>What IMD actually forecast.</h2></div></div>
    <div class="grid-2"><article class="g11-branch"><span class="g11-tag official-forecast">Official forecast</span><p class="eyebrow">Issued 17 September · valid 24–30 September</p><h3>Near-normal rain nationally for that week.</h3><p>IMD's extended-range bulletin forecast near-normal rainfall for India as a whole for Week 2. It does not forecast individual catchment supply, the dry season or the 2027 monsoon. <a href={weekForecast ? source(weekForecast.id) : url('/sources/')}>17 September bulletin ↗</a></p></article><article class="g11-branch"><span class="g11-tag official-forecast">Official forecast</span><p class="eyebrow">Issued 31 August · valid September 2026</p><h3>Below-normal rain was the monthly category.</h3><p>IMD's monthly outlook said all-India September rainfall was most likely below normal, its category for under 91% of the 1971–2020 September average. It also identified areas where normal or above-normal rain was possible. <a href={forecast ? source(forecast.id) : url('/sources/')}>31 August outlook ↗</a></p></article></div>
    <div class="g11-clock"><p class="eyebrow">Why these can differ</p><p>The 31 August document targets the whole of September; the 17 September issue targets 24–30 September. Neither is a measured end-of-month outcome or a prediction that taps will run dry. Their stated validity ends on 30 September, and any newer IMD bulletin should replace an active forecast card. <a href="https://mausam.imd.gov.in/">Check IMD's current bulletins ↗</a></p></div>
  </div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="shell reading"><p class="eyebrow">Exact official forecast records</p>{weekForecast && <ClaimBlock claim={weekForecast} heading="17 September issue · 24–30 September" />}{forecast && <ClaimBlock claim={forecast} heading="31 August issue · September 2026" />}</div></section>
</Site>
